---
title: Client Tools
id: client-tools
order: 4
description: "Client tools in TanStack AI run in the browser for UI updates, localStorage, and browser API access with automatic execution."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - client tools
  - browser tools
  - ui tools
  - automatic execution
  - clientTools
  - localStorage
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools execute in the browser, enabling UI updates, local storage access, and browser API interactions. Unlike server tools, client tools don't have an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute"},{"type":"text","value":" function in their server definition."}]},{"type":"code","value":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant LLM Service\n    participant Server\n    participant Browser\n    participant ClientTool\n    \n    LLM Service->>Server: tool_call chunk<br/>{name: \"updateUI\", args: {...}}\n    Server->>Server: Check if tool has<br/>server execute\n    \n    Note over Server: No execute function<br/>= client tool\n    \n    Server->>Browser: RUN_FINISHED client-tool<br/>interrupt via SSE/HTTP\n    Browser->>Browser: Find registered<br/>client tool\n    Browser->>ClientTool: execute(args)\n    ClientTool->>ClientTool: Update UI,<br/>localStorage, etc.\n    ClientTool-->>Browser: Return result\n    Browser->>Server: POST tool result\n    Server->>LLM Service: Add tool_result<br/>to conversation\n    \n    Note over LLM Service: Model uses result<br/>to continue\n    \n    LLM Service-->>Server: Stream response\n    Server-->>Browser: Forward chunks","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Use Client Tools"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"UI Updates"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local Storage"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Save user preferences, cache data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser APIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Access geolocation, camera, clipboard"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Update React/Vue/Solid state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Navigation"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Change routes, scroll to sections"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-it-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How It Works"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Call from LLM"}]},{"type":"text","value":": LLM decides to call a client tool"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Detection"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Server sees the tool has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute"},{"type":"text","value":" function"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Notification"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Server emits an internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client-tool-execution"},{"type":"text","value":"\npause on the interrupt wire (not a public item in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Execution"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The browser finds the registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nimplementation by tool name and runs it with the parsed input"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result Return"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Client auto-submits the result via the resume batch"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Update"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Successful output is validated. Execution and output\nvalidation failures are added as failed tool results."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LLM Continuation"}]},{"type":"text","value":": LLM receives the result and continues the conversation"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native client-tool execution shares the atomic interrupt "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"batch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" lifecycle\n(it can gate multi-item submits) but is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"auto-resolved"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — you do not call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" for it. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the\nephemeral lifecycle, batches, and migration from the historical\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-input-available"},{"type":"text","value":" custom event. After hydration, a pending client-tool\nexecution is restored but not run again. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor recovery policies."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"approval-is-a-separate-axis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approval is a separate axis"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A client tool can also require approval, and approval is separate from the\nbrowser result. Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and the tool pauses on a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-approval"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt first. You resolve "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"that decision only"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — once\napproved, the client runs the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation automatically and\nreturns its result:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) =>\n    interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n    interrupt.toolName === 'delete_local_data',\n)\n\nif (\n  approval?.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n  approval.toolName === 'delete_local_data'\n) {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You never resolve the execution by hand — that is what the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nimplementation is for. If you register a tool "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"without"}]},{"type":"text","value":" a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":"\nimplementation and want to supply the result yourself, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolResult"},{"type":"text","value":"\n(validated against the tool's output schema); it also preserves the historical\npath for legacy streams. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool approval flow"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for approval\nforms and "},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the approval lifecycle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defining-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defining Client Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools use the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":" API but with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" method:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// tools/definitions.ts - Shared between server and client\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport const saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    saved: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Client Tools"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-Side"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To give the LLM access to client tools, pass the tool definitions (not implementations) to the server when creating the chat:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// api/chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef } from \"./tools/definitions\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef], // Pass definitions\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Security:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" registering the definitions statically (as above) is the safe\ndefault — the server alone decides which tools the model sees, so a client\ncan't advertise tools you didn't sanction. If you'd instead like the client\nto declare its tools per request via AG-UI "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunAgentInput.tools"},{"type":"text","value":", use\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/ag-ui-compliance#tier-3--optional-let-the-client-advertise-its-tools","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mergeAgentTools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" —\nread its security note first, since "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params.tools"},{"type":"text","value":" is client-controlled."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create client implementations with automatic execution and full type safety:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/chat.tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages,\n  type ToolCallPart,\n  type MessagePart,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Step 1: Create client implementations (module scope)\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state - fully typed!\n  console.log(input.message, input.type);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorage = saveToLocalStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Step 2: A plain array is all you need — literal tool names, inputs and\n// outputs are inferred without any wrapper or `as const`.\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToLocalStorage];\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Step 3: Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof chatOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat(chatOptions);\n\n  // Step 4: Render with full type safety\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <MessageComponent key={message.id} message={message} />\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n// Messages component with full type safety\nfunction MessageComponent({ message }: { message: ChatMessages[number] }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {message.parts.map((part: MessagePart) => {\n        if (part.type === \"text\") {\n          return <p>{part.content}</p>;\n        }\n        \n        if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n          // ✅ part.name is narrowed to specific tool names\n          if (part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n            // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n            // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n            return (\n              <div>\n                Tool: {part.name}\n                {part.output && <span>✓ Success</span>}\n              </div>\n            );\n          }\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n    </div>\n  );\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-execution","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic Execution"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"automatically executed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when the model calls them. The flow is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LLM calls a client tool"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server sends a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client-tool-execution"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt to the browser"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client automatically executes the matching tool implementation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result is sent back to server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conversation continues with the result"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-tools-with-other-ag-ui-servers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools With Other AG-UI Servers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your agent runs on another AG-UI server, such as Pydantic AI. It calls a frontend tool, then ends the run. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" runs the tool and starts the next run with the result."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Register the client tools as in "},{"type":"link","href":"#client-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and point the connection at the endpoint of that server, for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents(\"/agent\")"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The AG-UI spec ends such a run with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" and a success outcome. The tool call gets no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT"},{"type":"text","value":". When that run ends:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client runs each tool call that the run started and did not answer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the server names the calls in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outcome.pendingToolCallIds"},{"type":"text","value":", the client runs only those of them that this run started."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The next request carries each result as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"role: \"tool\""},{"type":"text","value":" message."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If no client tool has the name of the call, the call does not run. If the arguments of the call are not complete JSON, the call does not run. A client tool with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" does not run on this path, because the server did not ask for approval."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"expose-client-tools-through-webmcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expose Client Tools Through WebMCP"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The normal client-tool path belongs to a TanStack chat run. It returns the browser result to the server and continues the conversation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP provides a separate execution path for browser agents. It calls the client handler and returns the result directly to WebMCP."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP execution does not call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolResult"},{"type":"text","value":". It does not continue a TanStack chat run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Register the same executable client tools with a framework lifecycle wrapper or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"registerWebMCPTools"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"./webmcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"WebMCP Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for setup and security rules."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-runtime-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Runtime Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools can receive typed runtime context as their second argument. This context is local to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" or framework hook instance and is not serialized to the server."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { toast } from \"./toast\";\n\nconst activeProjectId = \"\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  activeProjectId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst showToast = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"show_toast\",\n  description: \"Show a browser notification\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Project ${ctx.context.activeProjectId} updated`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [showToast],\n  context: {\n    activeProjectId,\n    toast: (message) => toast(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" for local browser dependencies. If the server also needs a value from the client, send it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forwardedProps"},{"type":"text","value":", validate it in your route, and map it into server "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat({ context })"},{"type":"text","value":" explicitly. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/runtime-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime Context"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-clienttools-helper-optional","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clientTools()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper (optional)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing a plain array — "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools: [toolA, toolB]"},{"type":"text","value":" — is all you need: tool names, inputs and outputs are inferred without any wrapper and without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"as const"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clientTools()"},{"type":"text","value":" is an optional identity helper that performs the same capture explicitly. Reach for it only when you want to build a shared, reusable tools tuple "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"outside"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the hook/options call:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { clientTools } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst notify = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify\",\n  description: \"Show a notification\",\n}).client(() => ({ ok: true }));\n\n// Equivalent to `const tools = [notify]` — just captured explicitly.\nconst tools = clientTools(notify);","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-safety-benefits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Safety Benefits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The isomorphic architecture provides complete end-to-end type safety:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nconst messages: UIMessage[] = [];\n\nmessages.forEach((message) => {\n  message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n    if (part.type === \"tool-call\" && part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n      // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally \"update_ui\"\n      // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n      // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n      \n      console.log(part.input.message); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      \n      if (part.output) {\n        console.log(part.output.success); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      }\n    }\n  });\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-states","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool States"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-call"},{"type":"text","value":" part moves through a small set of observable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallState"},{"type":"text","value":" values you can surface in the UI to indicate progress:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaiting-input"},{"type":"text","value":" — the model intends to call the tool but arguments haven't arrived yet."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-streaming"},{"type":"text","value":" — the model is streaming the tool arguments (partial input may be available)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-complete"},{"type":"text","value":" — all arguments have been received and the tool can run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-requested"},{"type":"text","value":" — the tool is waiting for user approval before it can run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-responded"},{"type":"text","value":" — the user has approved or denied the tool call."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallState"},{"type":"text","value":" union includes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" value, but the runtime never transitions a tool-call part to it — a finished call settles at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-complete"},{"type":"text","value":". Once the tool runs, the result appears two ways: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.output"},{"type":"text","value":" becomes populated on the tool-call part, and a sibling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-result"},{"type":"text","value":" part is emitted whose own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" (the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" case carries "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.error"},{"type":"text","value":"). Use the tool-call states for loading/streaming progress and the tool-result part for final success/error feedback."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { ToolCallPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction ToolCallDisplay({ part }: { part: ToolCallPart }) {\n  if (part.state === \"awaiting-input\") {\n    return <div>🔄 Waiting for arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-streaming\") {\n    return <div>📥 Receiving arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-complete\") {\n    return <div>✓ Arguments received, running tool...</div>;\n  }\n\n  // Completion shows up as a populated `part.output` (and as a sibling\n  // `tool-result` part whose state is `complete` / `error`).\n  if (part.output) {\n    return <div>✅ Tool complete</div>;\n  }\n  \n  return null;\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hybrid-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hybrid Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});\n\n// Server: Pass definition for client execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartDef] }); // Client will execute\n\n// Or pass server implementation for server execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartServer] }); // Server will execute","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"best-practices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep client tools simple"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Since client tools run in the browser, avoid heavy computations or large dependencies that could bloat your bundle size."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle errors gracefully"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Define clear error handling in your tool implementations and return meaningful error messages in your output schema."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update UI reactively"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Use your framework's state management (eg. React/Vue/Solid) to update the UI in response to tool executions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Secure sensitive data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Never store sensitive data (like API keys or personal info) in local storage or expose it via client tools."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide feedback"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Use tool states to inform users about ongoing operations and results of client tool executions (loading spinners, success messages, error alerts)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type everything"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Leverage TypeScript and Zod schemas for full type safety from tool definitions to implementations to usage."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-use-cases","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common Use Cases"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"UI Updates"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Local Storage"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Save user preferences, cache data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser APIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Access geolocation, camera, clipboard"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Update React/Vue/Solid state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Navigation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Change routes, scroll to sections"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Analytics"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Track user interactions"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How Tools Work"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Deep dive into the tool architecture"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Learn about server-side tool execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval Flow"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Add approval workflows for sensitive operations"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"when-to-use-client-tools","text":"When to Use Client Tools","level":2},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How It Works","level":2},{"id":"approval-is-a-separate-axis","text":"Approval is a separate axis","level":2},{"id":"defining-client-tools","text":"Defining Client Tools","level":2},{"id":"using-client-tools","text":"Using Client Tools","level":2},{"id":"server-side","text":"Server-Side","level":3},{"id":"client-side","text":"Client-Side","level":3},{"id":"automatic-execution","text":"Automatic Execution","level":2},{"id":"client-tools-with-other-ag-ui-servers","text":"Client Tools With Other AG-UI Servers","level":2},{"id":"expose-client-tools-through-webmcp","text":"Expose Client Tools Through WebMCP","level":2},{"id":"client-runtime-context","text":"Client Runtime Context","level":2},{"id":"the-clienttools-helper-optional","text":"The clientTools() helper (optional)","level":2},{"id":"type-safety-benefits","text":"Type Safety Benefits","level":2},{"id":"tool-states","text":"Tool States","level":2},{"id":"hybrid-tools","text":"Hybrid Tools","level":2},{"id":"best-practices","text":"Best Practices","level":2},{"id":"common-use-cases","text":"Common Use Cases","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next Steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nClient tools execute in the browser, enabling UI updates, local storage access, and browser API interactions. Unlike server tools, client tools don't have an `execute` function in their server definition.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant LLM Service\n    participant Server\n    participant Browser\n    participant ClientTool\n    \n    LLM Service->>Server: tool_call chunk<br/>{name: \"updateUI\", args: {...}}\n    Server->>Server: Check if tool has<br/>server execute\n    \n    Note over Server: No execute function<br/>= client tool\n    \n    Server->>Browser: RUN_FINISHED client-tool<br/>interrupt via SSE/HTTP\n    Browser->>Browser: Find registered<br/>client tool\n    Browser->>ClientTool: execute(args)\n    ClientTool->>ClientTool: Update UI,<br/>localStorage, etc.\n    ClientTool-->>Browser: Return result\n    Browser->>Server: POST tool result\n    Server->>LLM Service: Add tool_result<br/>to conversation\n    \n    Note over LLM Service: Model uses result<br/>to continue\n    \n    LLM Service-->>Server: Stream response\n    Server-->>Browser: Forward chunks\n```\n\n## When to Use Client Tools\n\n- **UI Updates**: Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility\n- **Local Storage**: Save user preferences, cache data\n- **Browser APIs**: Access geolocation, camera, clipboard\n- **State Management**: Update React/Vue/Solid state\n- **Navigation**: Change routes, scroll to sections\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. **Tool Call from LLM**: LLM decides to call a client tool\n2. **Server Detection**: Server sees the tool has no `execute` function\n3. **Client Notification**: Server emits an internal `client-tool-execution`\n   pause on the interrupt wire (not a public item in `interrupts`)\n4. **Client Execution**: The browser finds the registered `.client()`\n   implementation by tool name and runs it with the parsed input\n5. **Result Return**: Client auto-submits the result via the resume batch\n6. **Server Update**: Successful output is validated. Execution and output\n   validation failures are added as failed tool results.\n7. **LLM Continuation**: LLM receives the result and continues the conversation\n\nNative client-tool execution shares the atomic interrupt **batch** lifecycle\n(it can gate multi-item submits) but is **auto-resolved** — you do not call\n`resolveInterrupt` for it. See [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview) for the\nephemeral lifecycle, batches, and migration from the historical\n`tool-input-available` custom event. After hydration, a pending client-tool\nexecution is restored but not run again. See\n[Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools)\nfor recovery policies.\n\n## Approval is a separate axis\n\nA client tool can also require approval, and approval is separate from the\nbrowser result. Add `needsApproval: true` and the tool pauses on a\n`tool-approval` interrupt first. You resolve **that decision only** — once\napproved, the client runs the `.client()` implementation automatically and\nreturns its result:\n\n```ts ignore\nconst approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) =>\n    interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n    interrupt.toolName === 'delete_local_data',\n)\n\nif (\n  approval?.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n  approval.toolName === 'delete_local_data'\n) {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}\n```\n\nYou never resolve the execution by hand — that is what the `.client()`\nimplementation is for. If you register a tool **without** a `.client()`\nimplementation and want to supply the result yourself, use `addToolResult`\n(validated against the tool's output schema); it also preserves the historical\npath for legacy streams. See [Tool approval flow](./tool-approval) for approval\nforms and [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview) for the approval lifecycle.\n\n## Defining Client Tools\n\nClient tools use the same `toolDefinition()` API but with the `.client()` method:\n\n```typescript\n// tools/definitions.ts - Shared between server and client\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport const saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    saved: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});\n```\n\n## Using Client Tools\n\n### Server-Side\n\nTo give the LLM access to client tools, pass the tool definitions (not implementations) to the server when creating the chat:\n\n```typescript\n// api/chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef } from \"./tools/definitions\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef], // Pass definitions\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n> **Security:** registering the definitions statically (as above) is the safe\n> default — the server alone decides which tools the model sees, so a client\n> can't advertise tools you didn't sanction. If you'd instead like the client\n> to declare its tools per request via AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, use\n> [`mergeAgentTools`](../migration/ag-ui-compliance#tier-3--optional-let-the-client-advertise-its-tools) —\n> read its security note first, since `params.tools` is client-controlled.\n\n### Client-Side\n\nCreate client implementations with automatic execution and full type safety:\n\n```tsx\n// app/chat.tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages,\n  type ToolCallPart,\n  type MessagePart,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Step 1: Create client implementations (module scope)\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state - fully typed!\n  console.log(input.message, input.type);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorage = saveToLocalStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Step 2: A plain array is all you need — literal tool names, inputs and\n// outputs are inferred without any wrapper or `as const`.\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToLocalStorage];\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Step 3: Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof chatOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat(chatOptions);\n\n  // Step 4: Render with full type safety\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <MessageComponent key={message.id} message={message} />\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n// Messages component with full type safety\nfunction MessageComponent({ message }: { message: ChatMessages[number] }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {message.parts.map((part: MessagePart) => {\n        if (part.type === \"text\") {\n          return <p>{part.content}</p>;\n        }\n        \n        if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n          // ✅ part.name is narrowed to specific tool names\n          if (part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n            // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n            // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n            return (\n              <div>\n                Tool: {part.name}\n                {part.output && <span>✓ Success</span>}\n              </div>\n            );\n          }\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic Execution\n\nClient tools are **automatically executed** when the model calls them. The flow is:\n\n1. LLM calls a client tool\n2. Server sends a `client-tool-execution` interrupt to the browser\n3. Client automatically executes the matching tool implementation\n4. Result is sent back to server\n5. Conversation continues with the result\n\n## Client Tools With Other AG-UI Servers\n\nYour agent runs on another AG-UI server, such as Pydantic AI. It calls a frontend tool, then ends the run. `useChat` runs the tool and starts the next run with the result.\n\nRegister the client tools as in [Client-Side](#client-side), and point the connection at the endpoint of that server, for example `fetchServerSentEvents(\"/agent\")`.\n\nThe AG-UI spec ends such a run with `RUN_FINISHED` and a success outcome. The tool call gets no `TOOL_CALL_RESULT`. When that run ends:\n\n- The client runs each tool call that the run started and did not answer.\n- If the server names the calls in `outcome.pendingToolCallIds`, the client runs only those of them that this run started.\n- The next request carries each result as a `role: \"tool\"` message.\n\nIf no client tool has the name of the call, the call does not run. If the arguments of the call are not complete JSON, the call does not run. A client tool with `needsApproval: true` does not run on this path, because the server did not ask for approval.\n\n## Expose Client Tools Through WebMCP\n\nThe normal client-tool path belongs to a TanStack chat run. It returns the browser result to the server and continues the conversation.\n\nWebMCP provides a separate execution path for browser agents. It calls the client handler and returns the result directly to WebMCP.\n\nWebMCP execution does not call `addToolResult`. It does not continue a TanStack chat run.\n\nRegister the same executable client tools with a framework lifecycle wrapper or `registerWebMCPTools`. See [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp) for setup and security rules.\n\n## Client Runtime Context\n\nClient tools can receive typed runtime context as their second argument. This context is local to the `ChatClient` or framework hook instance and is not serialized to the server.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { toast } from \"./toast\";\n\nconst activeProjectId = \"\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  activeProjectId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst showToast = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"show_toast\",\n  description: \"Show a browser notification\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Project ${ctx.context.activeProjectId} updated`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [showToast],\n  context: {\n    activeProjectId,\n    toast: (message) => toast(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);\n```\n\nUse `context` for local browser dependencies. If the server also needs a value from the client, send it with `forwardedProps`, validate it in your route, and map it into server `chat({ context })` explicitly. See [Runtime Context](../advanced/runtime-context) for the full pattern.\n\n## The `clientTools()` helper (optional)\n\nPassing a plain array — `tools: [toolA, toolB]` — is all you need: tool names, inputs and outputs are inferred without any wrapper and without `as const`. `clientTools()` is an optional identity helper that performs the same capture explicitly. Reach for it only when you want to build a shared, reusable tools tuple **outside** the hook/options call:\n\n```ts\nimport { clientTools } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst notify = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify\",\n  description: \"Show a notification\",\n}).client(() => ({ ok: true }));\n\n// Equivalent to `const tools = [notify]` — just captured explicitly.\nconst tools = clientTools(notify);\n```\n\n## Type Safety Benefits\n\nThe isomorphic architecture provides complete end-to-end type safety:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nconst messages: UIMessage[] = [];\n\nmessages.forEach((message) => {\n  message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n    if (part.type === \"tool-call\" && part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n      // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally \"update_ui\"\n      // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n      // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n      \n      console.log(part.input.message); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      \n      if (part.output) {\n        console.log(part.output.success); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      }\n    }\n  });\n});\n```\n\n## Tool States\n\nA `tool-call` part moves through a small set of observable `ToolCallState` values you can surface in the UI to indicate progress:\n\n- `awaiting-input` — the model intends to call the tool but arguments haven't arrived yet.\n- `input-streaming` — the model is streaming the tool arguments (partial input may be available).\n- `input-complete` — all arguments have been received and the tool can run.\n- `approval-requested` — the tool is waiting for user approval before it can run.\n- `approval-responded` — the user has approved or denied the tool call.\n\nThe `ToolCallState` union includes a `complete` value, but the runtime never transitions a tool-call part to it — a finished call settles at `input-complete`. Once the tool runs, the result appears two ways: `part.output` becomes populated on the tool-call part, and a sibling `tool-result` part is emitted whose own `state` is `complete` or `error` (the `error` case carries `part.error`). Use the tool-call states for loading/streaming progress and the tool-result part for final success/error feedback.\n\n```tsx\nimport type { ToolCallPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction ToolCallDisplay({ part }: { part: ToolCallPart }) {\n  if (part.state === \"awaiting-input\") {\n    return <div>🔄 Waiting for arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-streaming\") {\n    return <div>📥 Receiving arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-complete\") {\n    return <div>✓ Arguments received, running tool...</div>;\n  }\n\n  // Completion shows up as a populated `part.output` (and as a sibling\n  // `tool-result` part whose state is `complete` / `error`).\n  if (part.output) {\n    return <div>✅ Tool complete</div>;\n  }\n  \n  return null;\n}\n```\n\n## Hybrid Tools\n\nTools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});\n\n// Server: Pass definition for client execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartDef] }); // Client will execute\n\n// Or pass server implementation for server execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartServer] }); // Server will execute\n```\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Keep client tools simple** - Since client tools run in the browser, avoid heavy computations or large dependencies that could bloat your bundle size.\n- **Handle errors gracefully** - Define clear error handling in your tool implementations and return meaningful error messages in your output schema.\n- **Update UI reactively** - Use your framework's state management (eg. React/Vue/Solid) to update the UI in response to tool executions.\n- **Secure sensitive data** - Never store sensitive data (like API keys or personal info) in local storage or expose it via client tools.\n- **Provide feedback** - Use tool states to inform users about ongoing operations and results of client tool executions (loading spinners, success messages, error alerts).\n- **Type everything** - Leverage TypeScript and Zod schemas for full type safety from tool definitions to implementations to usage.\n\n## Common Use Cases\n\n- **UI Updates** - Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility\n- **Local Storage** - Save user preferences, cache data\n- **Browser APIs** - Access geolocation, camera, clipboard\n- **State Management** - Update React/Vue/Solid state\n- **Navigation** - Change routes, scroll to sections\n- **Analytics** - Track user interactions\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [How Tools Work](./tool-architecture) - Deep dive into the tool architecture\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval) - Add approval workflows for sensitive operations\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Client Tools\nid: client-tools\norder: 4\ndescription: \"Client tools in TanStack AI run in the browser for UI updates, localStorage, and browser API access with automatic execution.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - client tools\n  - browser tools\n  - ui tools\n  - automatic execution\n  - clientTools\n  - localStorage\n---\n\nClient tools execute in the browser, enabling UI updates, local storage access, and browser API interactions. Unlike server tools, client tools don't have an `execute` function in their server definition.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant LLM Service\n    participant Server\n    participant Browser\n    participant ClientTool\n    \n    LLM Service->>Server: tool_call chunk<br/>{name: \"updateUI\", args: {...}}\n    Server->>Server: Check if tool has<br/>server execute\n    \n    Note over Server: No execute function<br/>= client tool\n    \n    Server->>Browser: RUN_FINISHED client-tool<br/>interrupt via SSE/HTTP\n    Browser->>Browser: Find registered<br/>client tool\n    Browser->>ClientTool: execute(args)\n    ClientTool->>ClientTool: Update UI,<br/>localStorage, etc.\n    ClientTool-->>Browser: Return result\n    Browser->>Server: POST tool result\n    Server->>LLM Service: Add tool_result<br/>to conversation\n    \n    Note over LLM Service: Model uses result<br/>to continue\n    \n    LLM Service-->>Server: Stream response\n    Server-->>Browser: Forward chunks\n```\n\n## When to Use Client Tools\n\n- **UI Updates**: Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility\n- **Local Storage**: Save user preferences, cache data\n- **Browser APIs**: Access geolocation, camera, clipboard\n- **State Management**: Update React/Vue/Solid state\n- **Navigation**: Change routes, scroll to sections\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. **Tool Call from LLM**: LLM decides to call a client tool\n2. **Server Detection**: Server sees the tool has no `execute` function\n3. **Client Notification**: Server emits an internal `client-tool-execution`\n   pause on the interrupt wire (not a public item in `interrupts`)\n4. **Client Execution**: The browser finds the registered `.client()`\n   implementation by tool name and runs it with the parsed input\n5. **Result Return**: Client auto-submits the result via the resume batch\n6. **Server Update**: Successful output is validated. Execution and output\n   validation failures are added as failed tool results.\n7. **LLM Continuation**: LLM receives the result and continues the conversation\n\nNative client-tool execution shares the atomic interrupt **batch** lifecycle\n(it can gate multi-item submits) but is **auto-resolved** — you do not call\n`resolveInterrupt` for it. See [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview) for the\nephemeral lifecycle, batches, and migration from the historical\n`tool-input-available` custom event. After hydration, a pending client-tool\nexecution is restored but not run again. See\n[Client persistence](../persistence/client-persistence#handle-restored-client-tools)\nfor recovery policies.\n\n## Approval is a separate axis\n\nA client tool can also require approval, and approval is separate from the\nbrowser result. Add `needsApproval: true` and the tool pauses on a\n`tool-approval` interrupt first. You resolve **that decision only** — once\napproved, the client runs the `.client()` implementation automatically and\nreturns its result:\n\n```ts ignore\nconst approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) =>\n    interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n    interrupt.toolName === 'delete_local_data',\n)\n\nif (\n  approval?.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n  approval.toolName === 'delete_local_data'\n) {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}\n```\n\nYou never resolve the execution by hand — that is what the `.client()`\nimplementation is for. If you register a tool **without** a `.client()`\nimplementation and want to supply the result yourself, use `addToolResult`\n(validated against the tool's output schema); it also preserves the historical\npath for legacy streams. See [Tool approval flow](./tool-approval) for approval\nforms and [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview) for the approval lifecycle.\n\n## Defining Client Tools\n\nClient tools use the same `toolDefinition()` API but with the `.client()` method:\n\n```typescript\n// tools/definitions.ts - Shared between server and client\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nexport const updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});\n\nexport const saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    saved: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n});\n```\n\n## Using Client Tools\n\n### Server-Side\n\nTo give the LLM access to client tools, pass the tool definitions (not implementations) to the server when creating the chat:\n\n```typescript\n// api/chat/route.ts\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef } from \"./tools/definitions\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json();\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.5\"),\n    messages,\n    tools: [updateUIDef, saveToLocalStorageDef], // Pass definitions\n  });\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n> **Security:** registering the definitions statically (as above) is the safe\n> default — the server alone decides which tools the model sees, so a client\n> can't advertise tools you didn't sanction. If you'd instead like the client\n> to declare its tools per request via AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, use\n> [`mergeAgentTools`](../migration/ag-ui-compliance#tier-3--optional-let-the-client-advertise-its-tools) —\n> read its security note first, since `params.tools` is client-controlled.\n\n### Client-Side\n\nCreate client implementations with automatic execution and full type safety:\n\n```tsx\n// app/chat.tsx\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { \n  createChatClientOptions, \n  type InferChatMessages,\n  type ToolCallPart,\n  type MessagePart,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst updateUIDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"update_ui\",\n  description: \"Update the UI with new information\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    message: z.string().meta({ description: \"Message to display\" }),\n    type: z.enum([\"success\", \"error\", \"info\"]).meta({ description: \"Message type\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ success: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorageDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"save_to_local_storage\",\n  description: \"Save data to browser local storage\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string().meta({ description: \"Storage key\" }),\n    value: z.string().meta({ description: \"Value to store\" }),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ saved: z.boolean() }),\n});\n\n// Step 1: Create client implementations (module scope)\nconst updateUI = updateUIDef.client((input) => {\n  // Update UI state - fully typed!\n  console.log(input.message, input.type);\n  return { success: true };\n});\n\nconst saveToLocalStorage = saveToLocalStorageDef.client((input) => {\n  localStorage.setItem(input.key, input.value);\n  return { saved: true };\n});\n\n// Step 2: A plain array is all you need — literal tool names, inputs and\n// outputs are inferred without any wrapper or `as const`.\nconst tools = [updateUI, saveToLocalStorage];\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools,\n});\n\n// Step 3: Infer message types for full type safety\ntype ChatMessages = InferChatMessages<typeof chatOptions>;\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, isLoading } = useChat(chatOptions);\n\n  // Step 4: Render with full type safety\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => (\n        <MessageComponent key={message.id} message={message} />\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n// Messages component with full type safety\nfunction MessageComponent({ message }: { message: ChatMessages[number] }) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {message.parts.map((part: MessagePart) => {\n        if (part.type === \"text\") {\n          return <p>{part.content}</p>;\n        }\n        \n        if (part.type === \"tool-call\") {\n          // ✅ part.name is narrowed to specific tool names\n          if (part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n            // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n            // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n            return (\n              <div>\n                Tool: {part.name}\n                {part.output && <span>✓ Success</span>}\n              </div>\n            );\n          }\n        }\n        return null;\n      })}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic Execution\n\nClient tools are **automatically executed** when the model calls them. The flow is:\n\n1. LLM calls a client tool\n2. Server sends a `client-tool-execution` interrupt to the browser\n3. Client automatically executes the matching tool implementation\n4. Result is sent back to server\n5. Conversation continues with the result\n\n## Client Tools With Other AG-UI Servers\n\nYour agent runs on another AG-UI server, such as Pydantic AI. It calls a frontend tool, then ends the run. `useChat` runs the tool and starts the next run with the result.\n\nRegister the client tools as in [Client-Side](#client-side), and point the connection at the endpoint of that server, for example `fetchServerSentEvents(\"/agent\")`.\n\nThe AG-UI spec ends such a run with `RUN_FINISHED` and a success outcome. The tool call gets no `TOOL_CALL_RESULT`. When that run ends:\n\n- The client runs each tool call that the run started and did not answer.\n- If the server names the calls in `outcome.pendingToolCallIds`, the client runs only those of them that this run started.\n- The next request carries each result as a `role: \"tool\"` message.\n\nIf no client tool has the name of the call, the call does not run. If the arguments of the call are not complete JSON, the call does not run. A client tool with `needsApproval: true` does not run on this path, because the server did not ask for approval.\n\n## Expose Client Tools Through WebMCP\n\nThe normal client-tool path belongs to a TanStack chat run. It returns the browser result to the server and continues the conversation.\n\nWebMCP provides a separate execution path for browser agents. It calls the client handler and returns the result directly to WebMCP.\n\nWebMCP execution does not call `addToolResult`. It does not continue a TanStack chat run.\n\nRegister the same executable client tools with a framework lifecycle wrapper or `registerWebMCPTools`. See [WebMCP Tools](./webmcp) for setup and security rules.\n\n## Client Runtime Context\n\nClient tools can receive typed runtime context as their second argument. This context is local to the `ChatClient` or framework hook instance and is not serialized to the server.\n\n```typescript\nimport { createChatClientOptions } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { toast } from \"./toast\";\n\nconst activeProjectId = \"\";\n\ntype ClientContext = {\n  activeProjectId: string;\n  toast(message: string): void;\n};\n\nconst showToast = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"show_toast\",\n  description: \"Show a browser notification\",\n}).client<ClientContext>((_input, ctx) => {\n  ctx.context.toast(`Project ${ctx.context.activeProjectId} updated`);\n  return { ok: true };\n});\n\nconst chatOptions = createChatClientOptions({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n  tools: [showToast],\n  context: {\n    activeProjectId,\n    toast: (message) => toast(message),\n  },\n});\n\nconst chat = useChat(chatOptions);\n```\n\nUse `context` for local browser dependencies. If the server also needs a value from the client, send it with `forwardedProps`, validate it in your route, and map it into server `chat({ context })` explicitly. See [Runtime Context](../advanced/runtime-context) for the full pattern.\n\n## The `clientTools()` helper (optional)\n\nPassing a plain array — `tools: [toolA, toolB]` — is all you need: tool names, inputs and outputs are inferred without any wrapper and without `as const`. `clientTools()` is an optional identity helper that performs the same capture explicitly. Reach for it only when you want to build a shared, reusable tools tuple **outside** the hook/options call:\n\n```ts\nimport { clientTools } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport { toolDefinition } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst notify = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"notify\",\n  description: \"Show a notification\",\n}).client(() => ({ ok: true }));\n\n// Equivalent to `const tools = [notify]` — just captured explicitly.\nconst tools = clientTools(notify);\n```\n\n## Type Safety Benefits\n\nThe isomorphic architecture provides complete end-to-end type safety:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nconst messages: UIMessage[] = [];\n\nmessages.forEach((message) => {\n  message.parts.forEach((part) => {\n    if (part.type === \"tool-call\" && part.name === \"update_ui\") {\n      // ✅ TypeScript knows part.name is literally \"update_ui\"\n      // ✅ part.input is typed as { message: string, type: \"success\" | \"error\" | \"info\" }\n      // ✅ part.output is typed as { success: boolean } | undefined\n      \n      console.log(part.input.message); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      \n      if (part.output) {\n        console.log(part.output.success); // ✅ Fully typed!\n      }\n    }\n  });\n});\n```\n\n## Tool States\n\nA `tool-call` part moves through a small set of observable `ToolCallState` values you can surface in the UI to indicate progress:\n\n- `awaiting-input` — the model intends to call the tool but arguments haven't arrived yet.\n- `input-streaming` — the model is streaming the tool arguments (partial input may be available).\n- `input-complete` — all arguments have been received and the tool can run.\n- `approval-requested` — the tool is waiting for user approval before it can run.\n- `approval-responded` — the user has approved or denied the tool call.\n\nThe `ToolCallState` union includes a `complete` value, but the runtime never transitions a tool-call part to it — a finished call settles at `input-complete`. Once the tool runs, the result appears two ways: `part.output` becomes populated on the tool-call part, and a sibling `tool-result` part is emitted whose own `state` is `complete` or `error` (the `error` case carries `part.error`). Use the tool-call states for loading/streaming progress and the tool-result part for final success/error feedback.\n\n```tsx\nimport type { ToolCallPart } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\n\nfunction ToolCallDisplay({ part }: { part: ToolCallPart }) {\n  if (part.state === \"awaiting-input\") {\n    return <div>🔄 Waiting for arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-streaming\") {\n    return <div>📥 Receiving arguments...</div>;\n  }\n  \n  if (part.state === \"input-complete\") {\n    return <div>✓ Arguments received, running tool...</div>;\n  }\n\n  // Completion shows up as a populated `part.output` (and as a sibling\n  // `tool-result` part whose state is `complete` / `error`).\n  if (part.output) {\n    return <div>✅ Tool complete</div>;\n  }\n  \n  return null;\n}\n```\n\n## Hybrid Tools\n\nTools can be implemented for both server and client, enabling flexible execution:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition, chat } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { db } from \"./db\";\n\n// Define once\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: \"add_to_cart\",\n  description: \"Add item to shopping cart\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n});\n\n// Server implementation - Store in database\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async (input) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.create({\n    data: { itemId: input.itemId, quantity: input.quantity },\n  });\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id };\n});\n\n// Client implementation - Update local wishlist\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client((input) => {\n  const wishlist = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"wishlist\") || \"[]\");\n  wishlist.push(input.itemId);\n  localStorage.setItem(\"wishlist\", JSON.stringify(wishlist));\n  return { success: true, cartId: \"local\" };\n});\n\n// Server: Pass definition for client execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartDef] }); // Client will execute\n\n// Or pass server implementation for server execution\nchat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages: [], tools: [addToCartServer] }); // Server will execute\n```\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Keep client tools simple** - Since client tools run in the browser, avoid heavy computations or large dependencies that could bloat your bundle size.\n- **Handle errors gracefully** - Define clear error handling in your tool implementations and return meaningful error messages in your output schema.\n- **Update UI reactively** - Use your framework's state management (eg. React/Vue/Solid) to update the UI in response to tool executions.\n- **Secure sensitive data** - Never store sensitive data (like API keys or personal info) in local storage or expose it via client tools.\n- **Provide feedback** - Use tool states to inform users about ongoing operations and results of client tool executions (loading spinners, success messages, error alerts).\n- **Type everything** - Leverage TypeScript and Zod schemas for full type safety from tool definitions to implementations to usage.\n\n## Common Use Cases\n\n- **UI Updates** - Show notifications, update forms, toggle visibility\n- **Local Storage** - Save user preferences, cache data\n- **Browser APIs** - Access geolocation, camera, clipboard\n- **State Management** - Update React/Vue/Solid state\n- **Navigation** - Change routes, scroll to sections\n- **Analytics** - Track user interactions\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [How Tools Work](./tool-architecture) - Deep dive into the tool architecture\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Tool Approval Flow](./tool-approval) - Add approval workflows for sensitive operations\n"}
